WN.lb(15,{"mt_yoI41SePkG":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Football shirt numbers are which type of data?","choices":["Categorical","Numerical and discrete","Numerical and continuous"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the mean of two shirt numbers mean anything?","explain":"Shirt numbers are labels for players. Their mean has no meaning, so they are categorical data."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Age, given in whole years, is which type of data?","choices":["Continuous","Discrete","Categorical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is age counted or measured? Does rounding change what it is?","explain":"Age is time, which is measured, so it is continuous. Rounding to whole years is only how we record it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"UK shoe sizes go 5, 5½, 6, 6½, and so on. What type of data is shoe size?","choices":["Discrete","Continuous","Categorical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you buy a size 5.3 shoe?","explain":"Shoe sizes come in set steps, with no sizes in between, so the data is discrete. Foot length would be continuous."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A vertical line chart shows numbers of siblings, with a line at 0, 1, 2, and so on, up to {n}. How many lines are on the chart?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count from 0, not from 1. Is 0 siblings one of the values?","explain":"The values are 0 to {n}, which is {=n+1} values. Counting from 0 adds one more than {n}.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why should the bars touch on a chart of grouped heights?","choices":["Heights are continuous, so every value between groups exists","Touching bars look neater","There are more than 4 groups"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 150.0 cm a real height? Which group is it in?","explain":"Height is continuous, so there are no gaps in real values. The class 150 ≤ h < 160 starts where the last one stops."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Times from 10 s up to {=10+5*k} s are grouped in classes of width 5 s (10 ≤ t < 15, and so on). How many classes are there?","answer":"k","hint":"Work out the range of times, then divide by the class width.","explain":"The range is {=5*k} s, and {=5*k} ÷ 5 = {k} classes.","params":{"k":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which variable gives discrete data?","choices":["Number of pets at home","Mass of a school bag","Length of a leaf"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one do you count, rather than measure?","explain":"Pets are counted in whole numbers, so the data is discrete. Mass and length are measured, so they are continuous."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"The temperature is read every hour for one day. Which chart suits it best?","choices":["A line graph","A pie chart","A bar chart with gaps"],"answer":0,"hint":"The temperature changes smoothly between the readings.","explain":"A line graph shows readings over time, and the line suggests the values in between. A pie chart needs parts of a whole."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Types of data: a survey records the reaction time of each pupil in seconds. What type of data is this?","choices":["Numerical and continuous","Numerical and discrete","Categorical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is time counted or measured? Could there be a value between 0.31 s and 0.32 s?","explain":"Time is measured, and 0.315 s lies between 0.31 s and 0.32 s. So reaction time is continuous numerical data."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A survey has {n} questions. 3 of them give categorical data. The rest give numerical data. How many questions give numerical data?","answer":"n-3","hint":"Every question gives either categorical or numerical data. Take the 3 categorical ones away from {n}.","explain":"{n} − 3 = {=n-3}. Each variable is one type or the other, so the rest are numerical.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which chart suits the favourite sport of each pupil in a class?","choices":["A bar chart with gaps","A histogram with touching bars","A line graph"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sports are separate names. Does a value exist between football and tennis?","explain":"Sports are categories with nothing in between, so the bars should have gaps. A pie chart would also work."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Heights from 130 cm up to {=130+10*k} cm are grouped in classes of width 10 cm (130 ≤ h < 140, and so on). How many classes are there?","answer":"k","hint":"Find the total range, then divide by the class width of 10 cm.","explain":"The range is {=10*k} cm, and {=10*k} ÷ 10 = {k} classes of width 10 cm.","params":{"k":[3,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A variable is one thing you collect data about, like eye colour or height. First ask: are the values names or numbers? Eye colour gives blue, brown and green. Those are names, so the data is categorical.","Some numbers are only labels. A football shirt number or a bus route number names something. A quick test helps: does a mean make sense? The mean of shirt 7 and shirt 9 is not a shirt 8 player. So shirt numbers are categorical too.","Numerical data splits two ways. Discrete data is counted, or it comes in set steps. You can have 2 or 3 siblings, but never 2.5. Continuous data is measured. Between 152.5 cm and 152.6 cm there is 152.55 cm, and you can keep zooming in. Height, mass, time and age are continuous.","Rounding does not change the type. We say age in whole years, but age is time, so it is continuous. Now pick the chart. Use a bar chart with gaps, or a pie chart, for categories. Use bars with gaps for discrete data. Use touching bars, a histogram, for grouped continuous data like heights in groups of 10 cm. Use a line graph for readings over time."],"adult":["This is KS3 statistics in the English national curriculum. Pupils draw and read tables, bar charts, pie charts and pictograms for categorical data. They use vertical line or bar charts for ungrouped and grouped numerical data. They compare discrete, continuous and grouped data. The same terms appear in Common Core grade 6 statistics, and GCSE assumes them.","Key terms: a variable is the thing you record. Categorical (qualitative) data are names or labels. Some are ordinal, with an order, like small, medium and large. Numerical (quantitative) data are amounts. Discrete data take separate values, and are often counts. Continuous data are measured, and can take any value in a range. Grouped continuous data uses class intervals written as inequalities, such as 150 ≤ h < 160. That way each value falls in exactly one class.","Common mistakes: calling any number numerical, when shirt and phone numbers are labels. Calling age or time discrete because it was rounded. Calling shoe size continuous, when sizes come in set steps (foot length is continuous). Drawing grouped heights with gaps between bars, as if 150.0 cm could not exist. Be careful with the word histogram. At KS3, touching bars with equal class widths are often called a frequency diagram. A true histogram, at GCSE Higher, plots frequency density.","In the lab, students sort variables with the mean test. They zoom into the gap between two heights, while the siblings line has nothing in between. They sort numbers into discrete or continuous, and try each chart on four data sets to see why it fits. Last, they run survey questions down a data tree."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, collect one bit of data about each person: favourite fruit, number of cousins, and hand span in cm. Ask which is categorical, which is discrete and which is continuous, and how you would draw each one.","keyIdea":"Data is categorical when the values are names or labels, and numerical when the values are amounts you can work with. Numerical data is discrete when it is counted or comes in set steps, and continuous when it is measured and can take any value in a range. The type decides the chart: gaps between bars for categories and discrete values, touching bars for grouped continuous data, and a line for readings over time.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What type of data is it? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🏷️ Categorical","🔢 Discrete","📏 Continuous"],"items":[["Favourite fruit",0],["Number of cousins",1],["Hand span in cm",2],["Bus route number",0],["Goals scored in a match",1],["Time to run 100 m",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdtype","caption":"Flagship lab: sort variables, zoom into the gap between two values, pick a chart for each data set, and use a data tree.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QPcvhTzMmD":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A dot plot of quiz scores has no dots above 7, but dots on both sides. What is the best description?","choices":["A gap: no one in this data scored 7","Scoring 7 is impossible","7 is an outlier"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what an empty spot inside the data means, and what it does not mean.","explain":"It is a gap. No one in this group scored 7, but another group could."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why do the bars of a histogram touch?","choices":["The classes join with no gaps between them","To save space on the page","Because the data is categorical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about times such as 29.9 s and 30 s. Is there any space between the classes?","explain":"Histograms show continuous (or grouped) numerical data. One class ends where the next starts, so the bars touch."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You record the heights of 300 pupils. Which display shows the shape most clearly?","choices":["A histogram with equal-width classes","A dot plot with one dot for each pupil","A histogram with a single 100 cm wide bar"],"answer":0,"hint":"300 dots make very tall stacks; one huge bar hides everything.","explain":"Grouping into sensible equal classes shows the distribution. 300 dots are too many, and one bar shows no shape."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Travel times cluster from 5 to 10 minutes, and one pupil took 45 minutes. What is 45?","choices":["An outlier","The peak","A gap"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 45 near the other values or far away from them?","explain":"45 is far from the cluster at 5–10, so it is an outlier. It is real data, so check it, but do not delete it without a reason."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Classes are 0 ≤ t < 10, 10 ≤ t < 20, 20 ≤ t < 30 with frequencies 4, 11, 6. What is the modal class?","choices":["10 ≤ t < 20","11","20 ≤ t < 30"],"answer":0,"hint":"The modal class is a class, not a number of values. Find the tallest bar.","explain":"11 is the highest frequency, so 10 ≤ t < 20 is the modal class."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The same 40 heights are drawn with classes 2 cm wide and with classes 20 cm wide. What happens with the 20 cm classes?","choices":["Only two bars, so the shape is hidden","More detail appears","The total frequency changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many 20 cm classes fit between 130 and 170 cm?","explain":"40 ÷ 20 = 2 bars. The total is still 40, but the peak and spread disappear."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A dot plot has {n} dots. {k} of them sit at the peak value. How many dots are at other values?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Each dot is one value. Take the dots at the peak away from the total.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} dots at other values.","params":{"n":[15,30],"k":[4,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Times are grouped into classes 10 s wide: 0 ≤ t < 10, 10 ≤ t < 20, and so on. Every time is less than {k} × 10 s, and some are just under it. How many classes do you need?","answer":"k","hint":"Each class covers 10 s. How many lots of 10 s reach from 0 up to {k} × 10 s?","explain":"The times run from 0 up to {=10*k} s, and {=10*k} ÷ 10 = {k}, so you need {k} classes and {k} touching bars.","params":{"k":[3,8]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A dot plot shows stacks of 2, 5, 3 and 1 dots above 0, 1, 2 and 3 pets. What is the mode?","choices":["1 pet","5 pets","3 pets"],"answer":0,"hint":"The mode is the value under the tallest stack, not the number of dots in it.","explain":"The tallest stack has 5 dots and it sits above 1 pet, so the mode is 1 pet."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A histogram of heights has {n} equal classes, each 5 cm wide. The first class starts at 130 cm. At what height in cm does the last class end?","answer":"130+5*n","hint":"Each class adds 5 cm. Work out how far {n} classes of 5 cm reach from 130.","explain":"{n} × 5 = {=5*n} cm, and 130 + {=5*n} = {=130+5*n} cm. Equal widths mean the classes step along evenly.","params":{"n":[3,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The classes are 20 ≤ t < 30 and 30 ≤ t < 40. A runner's time is exactly 30 s. Which class does it go in?","choices":["30 ≤ t < 40","20 ≤ t < 30","Both classes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the inequality signs. Which class can include 30 itself?","explain":"20 ≤ t < 30 stops just below 30. 30 ≤ t < 40 includes 30, so the time goes there, and only there."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A histogram has bars of frequency {a}, {b} and 6. How many values does it show in total?","answer":"a+b+6","hint":"Each bar height is the number of values in that class. Add them.","explain":"{a} + {b} + 6 = {=a+b+6} values altogether.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[3,12]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A dot plot is a number line with one dot for every value. Ten football matches give ten dots. Dots with the same value stack up, so the tallest stack shows the most common value: the mode, or peak.","Then read the shape. A cluster is a group of values close together. A gap is a stretch of the number line inside the data with no dots. An outlier is a value far away from the rest. A gap does not mean a value is impossible; it means no one in this data had it.","With 300 heights a dot plot becomes a forest of towers. A histogram groups the values into classes of equal width, such as 140 ≤ h < 150 cm, and draws one bar per class. The height of the bar is the frequency: how many values fall in that class.","The bars touch because the classes join with no gaps between them: a height can be 149.9 cm or 150 cm. Each value goes in exactly one class, and a boundary value goes in the class it starts. Very thin classes make a spiky picture; very wide ones hide the shape in a couple of bars."],"adult":["Curriculum links: Common Core 6.SP.4 asks students to display numerical data in plots on a number line, including dot plots, histograms and box plots, and 6.SP.2 and 6.SP.5 to describe a distribution by its centre, spread and overall shape. In England, KS3 statistics asks pupils to construct and interpret appropriate tables, charts and diagrams, including grouped frequency tables for continuous data, which leads to GCSE histograms (where unequal widths use frequency density; this lesson keeps to equal widths, so frequency is the bar height).","Key terms: distribution, frequency, class (class interval), class width, class boundary, mode and modal class, cluster, gap, peak, outlier, discrete and continuous data. Writing classes as inequalities such as 20 ≤ t < 30 makes it clear which class a boundary value belongs to.","Common mistakes: reading the height of a stack as the value (saying the peak is 6 when 6 is how many dots sit at 2 goals), treating a gap as an impossible value, putting a boundary value such as 30 s in the class that ends at 30, drawing gaps between histogram bars as if the data were categories, and being careful about class width, since very wide classes can hide two peaks.","In the lab students plot ten match results as stacked dots, find a peak, a gap and an outlier in travel times, change the class width of a histogram of 40 heights from 2 to 20 cm and compare shapes, drag bar tops to build a histogram from a frequency table, then read the modal class and place a boundary value."]},"familyTip":"Ask everyone at home how many minutes it took them to get ready this morning, and draw a dot plot on paper. Is there a cluster, a gap or an outlier? Then group the times into 5-minute classes and draw touching bars.","keyIdea":"A dot plot puts one dot for each value above a number line, so you can see every value and describe the shape: clusters, gaps, peaks and outliers. When there are many values, or the data is continuous, a histogram groups them into classes of equal width; the bar height is the frequency, and the bars touch because each class runs straight into the next (20 ≤ t < 30, then 30 ≤ t < 40). The class width you choose decides how much of the shape you can see.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which chart suits the data? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📍 Dot plot","📊 Histogram"],"items":[["Goals in 10 matches",0],["Heights of 300 pupils",1],["Shoe sizes of 15 friends",0],["Reaction times of 80 people",1],["Pets owned by 20 pupils",0],["Masses of 120 apples",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghist","caption":"Flagship lab: stack dots, find peaks, gaps and outliers, change class width, build a histogram from a table and read it.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_50bMWX7wRf":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why is How many legs does a spider have? not a statistical question?","choices":["Every answer is the same, 8","It is about animals","It has a number answer"],"answer":0,"hint":"A statistical question expects the answers to vary.","explain":"Spiders have 8 legs, so every answer is 8 and nothing varies. Number answers can still be statistical if they vary."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Data: 1, 2, 2, 3, 7. What is the median?","choices":["2","3","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sort the values and pick the middle one.","explain":"The middle of 1, 2, 2, 3, 7 is 2. The mean is 3 and the range is 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which change makes a question statistical?","choices":["How tall am I? becomes How tall are pupils in my class?","How tall am I? becomes How tall am I in metres?","How tall am I? becomes How tall was I last year?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a version where many different answers are expected.","explain":"Asking a whole class gives many heights that vary. The other versions still have one answer each."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Two data sets both have a mean of 10. Set P has range 2 and set Q has range 15. Which is more consistent?","choices":["P","Q","They are equally consistent"],"answer":0,"hint":"Consistent means the values are close together.","explain":"P's values sit within 2 of each other, so P is more consistent. The equal means hide the difference in spread."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Pupils' travel times to school range from {a} minutes to {b} minutes. What is the range?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Subtract the smallest time from the largest.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} minutes.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[15,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which describes the spread of a data set?","choices":["The range","The median","The mean"],"answer":0,"hint":"Centre is a typical value; spread is how far apart values are.","explain":"The range measures spread. The mean and median are measures of centre."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Five pupils each scored {s} on a quiz. What is the mean score?","answer":"s","hint":"If every value is the same, the typical value is that value.","explain":"Five scores of {s} add to {=5*s}, and {=5*s} ÷ 5 = {s}. The range is 0.","params":{"s":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Most dots on a plot sit at 1 and 2, with a few out at 9 and 10. What does this tell you about its shape?","choices":["It is skewed: most values are low with a long tail to the right","It is symmetric","It has no spread"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where are most of the dots, and where does the tail go?","explain":"The pile sits on the left and a tail stretches right, so the distribution is skewed, and the mean is pulled above the median."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A data set is {k}, {k}, {k}, {k}+{d}. What is the range?","answer":"d","hint":"Range is the largest value minus the smallest value.","explain":"Largest {=k+d} minus smallest {k} gives a range of {d}.","params":{"k":[2,6],"d":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is a statistical question?","choices":["How many minutes do pupils in Year 7 spend on homework each night?","How many minutes are in an hour?","How many minutes did I spend on homework last night?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Would different people, or different times, give different answers?","explain":"Homework time varies from pupil to pupil, so we expect variability. An hour always has 60 minutes, and one person on one night gives one answer."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A dot plot shows the number of pets owned by pupils. The smallest value is {a} and the largest is {a}+{b}. What is the range?","answer":"b","hint":"Range = largest value − smallest value.","explain":"Range = ({a}+{b}) − {a} = {b}. The range measures spread, not the size of the values.","params":{"a":[0,3],"b":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Class A scored 6, 7, 7, 8 and Class B scored 2, 5, 9, 12. Both have a mean of 7. What is different?","choices":["The spread: Class B's scores are more spread out","The centre: Class B scored higher","Nothing, the data is the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find each range: largest minus smallest.","explain":"Class A has range 2 and Class B has range 10. Same centre, very different spread."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Every one of {n} pupils is asked how many wheels a bicycle has, and each says 2. What is the range of the answers?","answer":"0","hint":"If every answer is the same, how far apart are the largest and smallest?","explain":"All {n} answers are 2, so the range is 2 − 2 = 0. No variability means the question is not statistical.","params":{"n":[5,30]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some questions have one fixed answer. How many days are in a week? Everyone says 7. Other questions get different answers from different people, like How many pets do you have? Those are statistical questions.","A statistical question expects variability. That means the answers vary. Having a number answer is not enough. How old am I? gives a number, but there is only one answer, so it is not statistical.","When you collect the answers, they form a distribution. You can show it on a dot plot. The centre is a typical value, like the mean or the median. The spread tells you how far apart the values are. The range is the largest value take away the smallest.","Two sets of data can share a centre but have very different spreads. Scores of 4, 5, 5, 5, 6 and scores of 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 both have a mean of 5. The first set is packed close together. The second is spread out."],"adult":["This topic matches Common Core 6.SP.1 and 6.SP.2: recognise a statistical question as one that anticipates variability in the data, and understand that a data set has a distribution described by its centre, spread and overall shape. In England the same ideas sit in Key Stage 3 statistics, where pupils describe and compare distributions using averages and the range.","Variability can come from asking many people (heights in a class) or from measuring one thing many times (how long my walk to school takes each day). The test is whether repeated answers are expected to differ, not what the question is about.","Common mistakes: thinking any question with a number answer is statistical. Calling a question about one person statistical because the topic, such as age, could vary. Using only the mean to compare two groups and ignoring spread. Mixing up the range with the largest value, or the median with the mean.","In the lab, learners sort six questions and collect answers onto dot plots to see a stack with range 0 next to a spread of answers. They drag dots to reach goals, such as range 0 with mean 5 or range 8 with the same mean, while mean, median and range update live. Last, they compare two classes by centre and spread."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, ask a question everyone answers, such as How many hours did you sleep last night? Write the answers down and talk about the middle value and how far apart the answers are. Then ask What day is it today? and notice why that one has no spread.","keyIdea":"A statistical question is one that expects variability in the answers: How many pets do pupils in my class have? is statistical, but How old am I? has one answer and is not. The data collected to answer a statistical question has a distribution, which can be described by its centre (such as the mean or median), its spread (such as the range) and its overall shape.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Statistical or not? Drag each question to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Statistical question","Not statistical"],"items":[["How tall are the pupils in my class?",0],["How tall is the school door?",1],["How many books did each pupil read this term?",0],["How many sides does a square have?",1],["What time do pupils go to bed?",0],["What year is it?",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagspread","caption":"Flagship lab: sort statistical questions, collect answers onto dot plots, drag dots to change centre and spread, then compare two classes.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_c29FaCTNsx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A tally has {g} gates of 5 and {s} more single lines. How many is that?","answer":"5*g+s","hint":"Count the gates in 5s, then count on the single lines.","explain":"{g} gates make {=5*g}. Count on {s} more to get {=5*g+s}.","params":{"g":[1,3],"s":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Each picture means 2 votes. Cats have 4 pictures. How many votes for cats?","choices":["8","4","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the key before you count.","explain":"Each picture is 2 votes, so count in 2s: 2, 4, 6, 8."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In a block diagram, which column shows the most popular?","choices":["The tallest one","The first one","The shortest one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each block is one vote, so more blocks means more votes.","explain":"The tallest column has the most blocks, so it has the most votes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Walk has 6 votes. Bus has 2. How many more for walk?","choices":["4","8","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many more means find the difference, not add.","explain":"6 take away 2 is 4, so 4 more children walk."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Each picture means 2 votes. What does half a picture mean?","choices":["1 vote","2 votes","Half a child"],"answer":0,"hint":"A whole picture is 2 votes. What is half of 2?","explain":"Half of 2 is 1, so half a picture means 1 vote."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Apples got {a} votes and pears got {b} votes. How many votes in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"In all means add the two totals together.","explain":"{a} and {b} make {=a+b} votes in all.","params":{"a":[3,8],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why do we cross the 5th tally mark?","choices":["So we can count in 5s","To show a mistake","Because 5 is the end"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about counting lots of tally marks quickly.","explain":"A gate of 5 lets you count 5, 10, 15 instead of counting every line."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Each picture means 1 vote. Bananas have 3 pictures. How many tally marks should bananas have?","choices":["3","1","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"A tally chart and a pictogram show the same counts.","explain":"One picture for each vote, so 3 pictures match 3 tally marks."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How do you draw 5 with tally marks?","choices":["4 lines and 1 line across them","5 lines standing up","The number 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"The 5th line does something different from the first 4.","explain":"The 5th line goes across the other 4. That makes a gate of 5 that is quick to count."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Each picture in a pictogram means 2 votes. A row has {n} pictures. How many votes is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count in 2s, once for each picture.","explain":"{n} pictures of 2 votes each is {=2*n} votes. Count in 2s.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A block diagram shows {a} children like red and {b} like blue. How many more like red than blue?","answer":"a-b","hint":"How many more means find the difference. Take the smaller number from the bigger one.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}, so {=a-b} more children like red.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Each picture means 2 votes. A row has 3 whole pictures and a half picture. How many votes?","choices":["7","4","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count in 2s for the whole pictures. Half of 2 is 1.","explain":"3 whole pictures are 2, 4, 6, and the half picture is 1 more, so 7 votes."}],"explain":{"kid":["Tally marks help you count. Draw one line for each vote. The 5th line goes across the other 4. That makes a gate. A gate is 5.","A pictogram uses pictures. Look at the key first. If one picture means 1, count the pictures. If one picture means 2, count in 2s. Half a picture means 1.","A block diagram has one block for each vote. The tallest column has the most votes. The shortest has the fewest.","How many more? Find the difference. Walk 6, car 4. Take 4 from 6. That is 2 more."],"adult":["In England, Year 2 statistics asks children to interpret and construct simple pictograms, tally charts, block diagrams and simple tables, to ask and answer simple questions by counting the number of objects in each category and sorting the categories by quantity, and to ask and answer questions about totalling and comparing categorical data. Year 3 moves to pictograms where one symbol stands for more than one, such as 2, 5 or 10. In the US, Common Core 2.MD.D.10 asks for picture graphs and bar graphs with a single-unit scale.","The ideas underneath: a tally is a running count, and the gate of five lets you count in 5s instead of in 1s. In a pictogram the key is the scale. With a key of 2, a row of 4 pictures means 8, and half a picture means 1. A block diagram is an early bar chart: each block is one item, so the height of a column shows its total, and two columns can be compared block by block.","Common mistakes to watch for: drawing five upright lines instead of four and a line across; counting the pictures and forgetting the key, so 4 pictures read as 4 instead of 8; treating half a picture as a whole one; adding the two numbers when asked how many more; and reading the most popular as the first row rather than the longest one.","In the lab your child tallies 11 fruit votes into gates of 5, turns the tally into a pictogram, builds a pictogram where each picture means 2 with a half picture for 1, stacks a block diagram from a tally, and finds how many more by spotting the blocks with no partner. Wrong taps get feedback that names the slip."]},"familyTip":"Count something together this week, such as the colours of cars that pass or the fruit in the bowl, and keep a tally on paper in gates of 5. Then draw it as a pictogram and ask: which had the most, and how many more than the least?","keyIdea":"A tally chart counts as you go: one line for each thing, and the 5th line goes across the other 4 to make a gate of 5, so you can count in 5s. A pictogram shows the same data with pictures. The key tells you what one picture means: if each picture means 2, count in 2s, and half a picture means 1. A block diagram stacks one block for each thing, so the tallest column is the most popular. To find how many more, find the difference between two totals: take the smaller from the bigger.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Tally chart, pictogram or block diagram? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Tally chart","Pictogram","Block diagram"],"items":[["Lines in gates of 5",0],["Count as each vote comes in",0],["Pictures in rows",1],["Has a key: each picture means 2",1],["One block for each vote, in columns",2],["The tallest column has the most",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtally","caption":"Flagship lab: tally votes in gates of 5, build pictograms with keys of 1 and 2, stack a block diagram and find how many more.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_R-CEmul2Ws":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a census?","choices":["Collecting data from every member of the population","Asking a random sample","A kind of graph"],"answer":0,"hint":"The UK holds one every ten years and asks every household.","explain":"A census collects data from the whole population. A sample collects data from part of it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What makes a sample random?","choices":["Every member has the same chance of being picked","You pick whoever is nearby","People choose to join"],"answer":0,"hint":"Random in statistics means fair chance, not just any old way.","explain":"In a random sample every member of the population has the same chance of being chosen, so no group is left out on purpose."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"In a random sample of 50 people, {y} like a new park design. Estimate how many of 1,000 residents like it.","answer":"y*20","hint":"1,000 is 20 times 50, so scale the sample count up by 20.","explain":"{y} × 20 = {=y*20}, so about {=y*20} of the 1,000 residents like the design.","params":{"y":[10,40]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Random samples of 10 students say {a}% and {b}% yes. What is the range of these two estimates, in percentage points?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Subtract the smaller estimate from the larger one.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} percentage points. Small samples vary a lot, so a bigger sample would give a smaller spread.","params":{"a":[60,80],"b":[20,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"To find how much exercise teenagers in a town do, Ali asks people leaving the gym. What is the problem?","choices":["People at the gym exercise more than most","He should ask adults","The sample is random"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the gym crowd like all the teenagers in the town?","explain":"Gym users are not representative of all teenagers: they exercise more, so the sample is biased upward."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which sample of a 1,200-student school is likely to be best?","choices":["100 students picked at random from the full list","400 students from Year 11","The first 100 to arrive"],"answer":0,"hint":"A bigger sample from one year group is still only one year group.","explain":"100 picked at random from the full list is representative. 400 from Year 11 is bigger but biased towards one year."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Two random samples of 40 students give 45% and 55%. What does this show?","choices":["Random samples vary; the true value is probably near 50%","One sample must be biased","Sampling never works"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do two fair samples have to give the same answer?","explain":"This is sampling variability. Fair samples differ a little, and both are close to the true value, which is probably near 50%."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why might a company test 50 light bulbs from a batch instead of all of them?","choices":["Testing destroys the bulbs, so a census would leave none to sell","Samples are always more accurate","Bulbs cannot be counted"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to a bulb when you test how long it lasts?","explain":"Testing until a bulb fails uses it up, so a census is impossible here. A random sample of 50 estimates the whole batch."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A school asks 60 of its 900 students about lunch. What is the population?","choices":["All 900 students","The 60 students asked","The lunch menu"],"answer":0,"hint":"The population is the whole group you want to know about.","explain":"The school wants to know about all 900 students, so they are the population. The 60 asked are the sample."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"In a random sample of 20 students, {y} say yes. What percentage of the sample is that?","answer":"y*5","hint":"Each student is 1 out of 20, which is 5%.","explain":"{y} out of 20 is {y} × 5% = {=y*5}%, so the estimate for the whole population is about {=y*5}% yes.","params":{"y":[4,16]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A random sample says {p}% of students walk to school. The school has 600 students. About how many walk?","answer":"p*6","hint":"1% of 600 is 6, so multiply the percentage by 6.","explain":"{p}% of 600 = {p} × 6 = {=p*6}, so about {=p*6} students walk. It is an estimate, because the sample can be a bit off.","params":{"p":[20,80]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A website poll lets anyone vote, and 2,000 people do. Why could it be biased?","choices":["People who care most choose to vote","2,000 is too few","Websites cannot count votes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about who decides to answer a poll that anyone can join.","explain":"A voluntary poll is answered by people with strong views, so it leans one way however many vote."}],"explain":{"kid":["The population is everyone you want to know about. If you want to know what Hilltop students think, the population is all 240 of them. Asking all of them is called a census.","A census can take too long or cost too much, so we often ask a sample: a smaller group taken from the population. Then we use the sample to estimate the answer for everyone.","A sample only tells the truth if it is like the population. Asking only your friends, one class or people who choose to answer gives a biased sample. It leans one way.","In a random sample, every person has the same chance of being picked. Random samples still differ a bit from each other, but they scatter around the true answer. Bigger random samples land closer. A bigger biased sample is still biased."],"adult":["This matches the US Common Core standard 7.SP.A.1: a sample can be used to generalise about a population only if it is representative, and random sampling tends to give representative samples. It also matches the GCSE Mathematics content in England, which asks pupils to infer properties of a population from a sample while knowing the limits of sampling.","Key terms are population, census, sample, random sample, representative and bias. Sampling variability is the natural spread between random samples of the same size. It shrinks as the sample grows, which is why larger random samples give steadier estimates.","Common mistakes: thinking a big sample is always a good one; trusting a voluntary response poll because many people answered; confusing the population with the sample; and expecting a random sample to give the exact true value. Be careful with the word random, which in daily speech means haphazard, not a fair chance for all.","In the lab your learner sorts groups into population and sample, drags a box to take handy samples from one corner of a school, draws random samples that scatter round the true 50 percent, compares sample sizes with a big but biased sample, and judges four ways of choosing students."]},"familyTip":"When you see a poll in the news or online, ask two questions together: who was the population, and how was the sample chosen? A phone-in or website poll lets people choose to answer, so it is biased. A good poll says how many were asked and that they were chosen at random.","keyIdea":"The population is the whole group you want to know about. A census asks all of it; a sample asks part of it and uses the answers to estimate the whole. A sample only works if it is representative. A random sample gives everyone the same chance and scatters around the truth. A biased sample, such as volunteers or one handy group, leans one way, and making it bigger does not fix that.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"A survey of all the students in a school: is each sample random, or biased? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Random sample","Biased sample"],"items":[["30 names drawn by a computer from the full school list",0],["Every 10th name on the full register",0],["The first 30 students through the gate",1],["Students who reply to an email asking for volunteers",1],["Everyone in the chess club",1],["Numbers picked from a hat with one number per student",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsample","caption":"Flagship lab: sort population and sample, drag a handy sample box, take random samples of different sizes, and judge four ways of choosing.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_xppl18avyY":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"4 animals and 3 vehicles. Animals count is…","choices":["4","3","7"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the tally for the animals box only.","explain":"The animals category has 4 members, so its count is 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Most to least here is…","choices":["Animals then Vehicles","Vehicles then Animals","Same size"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare 4 with 3, then list bigger first.","explain":"Animals (4) outnumber vehicles (3), so animals come first."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is a mix-up?","choices":["Sort but never count the groups","Use the given boxes","Order groups by count"],"answer":0,"hint":"Skipping the tally breaks K.MD.3.","explain":"Sorting without counting leaves out the required tallies."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sorting into categories means…","choices":["use given groups, then count","only invent a colour rule","never compare group sizes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Labels are given; counts come next.","explain":"Classify into given categories, then count and compare."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Category A has {n} items. What tally do you write?","answer":"n","hint":"Write the number of items you counted.","explain":"The tally for that category is {=n}.","params":{"n":[1,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Two categories have {a} and {b}. What is the total sorted?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add both category counts together.","explain":"All sorted objects total {=a+b} when groups are {a} and {b}.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"3 red and 5 blue. Most is…","choices":["blue (5)","red (3)","neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"Larger tally wins when ordering by count.","explain":"Blue’s count of 5 is greater than red’s 3."},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: finish a category sort.","items":["read the given labels","sort every object","say which group has more"],"hint":"Labels, then place, then compare counts.","explain":"Use given categories, classify all items, then compare tallies."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A bus goes in…","choices":["Vehicles","Animals","Either box"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the given category name that fits a bus.","explain":"A bus is a vehicle, so it belongs in the Vehicles category."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"After sorting you should…","choices":["count each box","throw the boxes away","only look at colours"],"answer":0,"hint":"K.MD.3 asks for counts in each category.","explain":"Classify first, then count the objects in each category."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A box holds {n} toys. What is the count for that category?","answer":"n","hint":"The count equals how many objects sit in the box.","explain":"If the category has {n} objects, its count is {=n}.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Category A has {a} and B has {b} (A is larger). How many more in A?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Subtract the smaller tally from the larger tally carefully.","explain":"When A has {a} and B has {b}, A has {=a-b} more.","params":{"a":[5,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order the sorting steps.","items":["put items in given boxes","count each category","order categories by count"],"hint":"Classify, then tally, then compare sizes.","explain":"Given categories first, then counts, then order by those counts."}],"explain":{"kid":["Someone gives you the boxes. Put each thing in the right box.","Count how many are in each box. Say the number out loud.","Which box has more? Put the bigger group first when you order by count.","Sorting is not finished until you can say how many are in each category."],"adult":["K.MD.3: put objects into given groups, count each group, then order the groups by count. Words: group, count, most, least.","Common mistakes: ignoring the given labels; sorting but never counting; comparing without counts; mixing this up with free colour sorting.","In the lab your child sorts animals and vehicles, sets counts 4 and 3, orders most to least, and spots sorting without counting."]},"familyTip":"Give a mixed pile of toys with two labels (animals / vehicles). Sort, count each box, then say which has more.","keyIdea":"Put objects into the given groups, count how many are in each group, then order the groups from most to least.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fair sorting idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["put each item in a given box",0],["count how many in each box",0],["order groups from most to least",0],["never count after sorting",1],["ignore the given labels",1],["compare category sizes",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcatct","caption":"Flagship lab: sort into Animals/Vehicles, count each, order by count, catch mix-ups.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_NWUGQ7J3Or":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why take the absolute value when finding deviations for MAD?","choices":["so distances above and below the mean do not cancel","so the MAD equals the range","so the MAD equals the mean"],"answer":0,"hint":"Without absolute value, signed deviations sum to zero about the mean.","explain":"Absolute value keeps distance. Signed deviations about the mean cancel; MAD would wrongly become 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["MAD is the same as the range","MAD averages |value − mean|","Same mean can hide different MAD"],"answer":0,"hint":"Range uses only the ends; MAD uses every value.","explain":"Range = max − min. MAD averages absolute deviations from the mean — different measure."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Data 3, 5, 7. Mean is 5. Absolute deviations are…","choices":["2, 0, 2","3, 5, 7","0, 0, 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compute |3−5|, |5−5| and |7−5| carefully.","explain":"|3−5|=2, |5−5|=0, |7−5|=2. Then MAD = (2+0+2)/3 = 4/3."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Absolute deviations 1, 1, 1, {a}. MAD = (1+1+1+{a})/4. Enter that MAD when a={a}.","answer":"(3+a)/4","hint":"Add the four absolute deviations, then divide by 4.","explain":"MAD = (3+{a})/4 = {=(3+a)/4}.","params":{"a":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A bigger MAD means…","choices":["values sit farther from the mean on average","the mean is larger","the range must be zero"],"answer":0,"hint":"MAD measures typical distance from the centre.","explain":"Larger MAD → typically farther from the mean. It does not force the mean to grow or the range to vanish."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Correct MAD steps start with…","choices":["find the mean → absolute deviations → average them","find the range → call it MAD","add the raw values and stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Centre first, then distances, then average.","explain":"Mean, then |x − mean| for each x, then average those absolute deviations."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Data {a}, {a}+2. Mean = {a}+1. Absolute deviations are both 1. MAD?","answer":"1","hint":"Two absolute deviations of 1 each — average them.","explain":"MAD = (1+1)/2 = 1. Both values sit 1 unit from the mean {a}+1.","params":{"a":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Range of 2, 5, 9 is 7. MAD of the same set is…","choices":["not necessarily 7 — range and MAD are different measures","always equal to the range","always equal to the mean"],"answer":0,"hint":"Range uses ends only; MAD averages every absolute deviation.","explain":"Mean = 16/3. Absolute deviations differ from 7. Range ≠ MAD in general."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"{n} identical absolute deviations of {d} each. What is the MAD?","answer":"d","hint":"Average of n copies of d is d.","explain":"MAD = ({n}×{d})/{n} = {d}.","params":{"n":[3,8],"d":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The MAD is…","choices":["the average of the absolute deviations from the mean","the largest value minus the smallest","another name for the mean"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think: average of |value − mean|, not the range.","explain":"MAD = mean of |each value − mean|. Range uses only the extremes; the mean is the centre, not the spread."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Four absolute deviations from the mean are {a}, {a}, {a} and {a}. What is the MAD?","answer":"a","hint":"Average four equal absolute deviations — the average equals each one.","explain":"MAD = ({a}+{a}+{a}+{a})/4 = {a}. Equal absolute deviations average to that same value.","params":{"a":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Set A: 5, 5, 5. Set B: 1, 5, 9. Both have mean 5. Which has the larger MAD?","choices":["Set B","Set A","They must have the same MAD because the means match"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same centre can hide different typical distances from that centre.","explain":"Set A has MAD 0. Set B's values sit farther from 5, so MAD is larger. Same mean ≠ same MAD."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Values {a}, {a}, {a}. Absolute deviations from the mean are all 0. What is the MAD?","answer":"0","hint":"If every value equals the mean, every absolute deviation is 0.","explain":"All values are {a}, so mean = {a} and MAD = 0. Zero MAD means no spread about the mean.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Four absolute deviations from the mean are {a}, {a}, {b}, {b}. What is the MAD?","answer":"(a+a+b+b)/4","hint":"MAD is the average of the absolute deviations.","explain":"MAD = ({a}+{a}+{b}+{b})/4 = {=(a+a+b+b)/4}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["First find the mean of your data. Then for each value, find how far it is from that mean — ignore whether it was above or below. That distance is the absolute deviation.","Add those absolute deviations and divide by how many values you have. The result is the mean absolute deviation, or MAD. It tells you the typical distance from the mean.","Two data sets can share the same mean but have different MAD. Scores 4, 5, 5, 6 and scores 1, 5, 5, 9 both have mean 5, but the second set is more spread out so its MAD is larger.","MAD is not the range (largest minus smallest) and not the mean itself. Range uses only the ends; MAD uses every value's distance from the centre."],"adult":["CCSS 6.SP.5c: use centre and spread (including MAD) with the shape of the data. England KS3: find and read averages and spread. Not the same as Statistical questions and variability (flagspread) or Dot plots and histograms (flaghist).","Words: mean, absolute deviation, MAD, spread. Common mistakes: calling MAD the range; thinking MAD is the mean; forgetting absolute value so signed gaps cancel; averaging only the biggest gap.","In the lab your child finds the mean, marks each |x − mean|, averages them into the MAD, compares two same-mean sets, spots mean/range mix-ups, and practises the steps."]},"familyTip":"With five dinner times or phone-battery percentages, find the mean together, then the absolute distance of each number from that mean, then average those distances — that average is the MAD.","keyIdea":"The mean absolute deviation (MAD) is the average of how far each value sits from the mean: for each value find |value − mean|, then average those absolute deviations. Same mean can hide very different MAD; MAD measures typical distance from the centre, not the range and not the mean itself.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"MAD idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True MAD idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["MAD averages |value − mean|",0],["Two sets can share a mean but have different MAD",0],["MAD is the same as the range",1],["MAD is just another name for the mean",1],["Absolute deviation drops the sign of the distance",0],["A bigger MAD means values sit farther from the mean on average",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmad","caption":"Flagship lab: find the mean, mark absolute deviations, average them into MAD, compare two same-mean sets, catch mean/range mix-ups, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Cqm8iy48UI":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A train leaves at 14:05. What is that in 12-hour time?","choices":["2:05 pm","4:05 pm","14:05 am"],"answer":0,"hint":"For times after 12:00, take away 12 hours.","explain":"14 − 12 = 2, so 14:05 is 2:05 in the afternoon, written 2:05 pm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A train leaves at 11:{a} and arrives at 12:{b}. How many minutes is the trip?","answer":"60-a+b","hint":"Jump to 12:00 first, then count on the rest.","explain":"11:{a} to 12:00 is {=60-a} minutes, and 12:00 to 12:{b} is {b} minutes, so the trip is {=60-a+b} minutes.","params":{"a":[40,58],"b":[10,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Bus 1 leaves at 08:45 and arrives at 09:16. Sam says the trip is 71 minutes because 916 − 845 = 71. What is wrong?","choices":["an hour has 60 minutes, so the trip is 31 minutes","nothing, it is 71 minutes","he should add the times"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count on from 08:45 to 09:00, then to 09:16.","explain":"08:45 to 09:00 is 15 minutes and 09:00 to 09:16 is 16 minutes, so 31 minutes. Taking 845 from 916 treats an hour as 100 minutes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"In a two-way table, the Netball column has {a} in Year 5 and {b} in Year 6. What is the Netball total?","answer":"a+b","hint":"A column total adds up all the cells above it.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. The total at the bottom of a column is the sum of that column.","params":{"a":[4,15],"b":[4,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You reach the Library at 09:40. The Library row says 08:58, 09:33, 10:18, 11:52. When is the next bus?","choices":["10:18","09:33","11:52"],"answer":0,"hint":"The next bus is the first time after 09:40.","explain":"08:58 and 09:33 have already gone. The first time after 09:40 is 10:18."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You must be at the Library by 09:40. Bus 1 gets there at 08:58, Bus 2 at 09:33 and Bus 3 at 10:18. Which is the latest bus you can catch?","choices":["Bus 2","Bus 1","Bus 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Latest means the last one that still gets there in time.","explain":"Bus 1 and Bus 2 both arrive before 09:40, but Bus 2 is later, so it is the latest one that works. Bus 3 arrives at 10:18, too late."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A two-way table has a Total column with {a} for Year 5 and {b} for Year 6. What is the grand total in the bottom corner?","answer":"a+b","hint":"The grand total adds up the Total column. The Total row gives the same number.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. Adding the Total row would give {=a+b} too, which is a good check.","params":{"a":[20,40],"b":[20,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In a timetable, Bus 3 reaches the Library at 10:18 and Market Square at 10:27. When does it reach Market Square?","choices":["10:27","10:18","10:05"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find Bus 3's column, then the Market Square row.","explain":"10:27 is in the Market Square row. 10:18 is the Library row, and 10:05 is when it leaves the first stop."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In a bus timetable, each column is one bus and each row is one stop. How do you find when Bus 3 reaches the Station?","choices":["go down Bus 3's column to the Station row","go along the Station row and pick the first time","go down Bus 3's column and pick the last time you see"],"answer":0,"hint":"You need the cell that is in both Bus 3's column and the Station row.","explain":"The answer is in the cell where Bus 3's column meets the Station row. The first time in the Station row is a different bus."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A bus leaves at 9:{a} and arrives at 10:{b}. How many minutes does the trip take?","answer":"60-a+b","hint":"Count on from 9:{a} to 10:00 first. Then count on to 10:{b}.","explain":"From 9:{a} to 10:00 is {=60-a} minutes. From 10:00 to 10:{b} is {b} minutes. {=60-a} + {b} = {=60-a+b} minutes.","params":{"a":[35,55],"b":[10,25]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"In a two-way table, the Year 6 row has {a} for football, {b} for swimming and a gap for netball. The row total is {t}. What number goes in the gap?","answer":"t-a-b","hint":"The row total is all the cells in the row added up. Take the cells you know away from it.","explain":"{t} − {a} − {b} = {=t-a-b}. Check: {a} + {b} + {=t-a-b} = {t}.","params":{"a":[5,15],"b":[5,15],"t":[40,60]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A timetable shows a dash (—) for Bus 2 at Market Square. What does it mean?","choices":["Bus 2 does not stop at Market Square","Bus 2 waits at Market Square","the time was left out by mistake"],"answer":0,"hint":"Timetables use a dash on purpose.","explain":"A dash means the bus does not stop there. You need to find the next bus that has a time in that row."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You must be at the Station by 10:45. Bus 3 gets there at 10:36 and Bus 4 gets there at 12:14. Which bus should you catch?","choices":["Bus 3","Bus 4","either one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which bus arrives before 10:45?","explain":"Bus 3 arrives at 10:36, before 10:45. Bus 4 arrives at 12:14, which is too late."}],"explain":{"kid":["A table puts facts in rows and columns. Rows go across. Columns go down. Each box, or cell, sits in one row and one column.","In a bus timetable, each column is one bus. Each row is one stop. To find when a bus gets to a stop, go down that bus's column. Stop at the row for your stop. Read the time in that cell. A dash (—) means the bus does not stop there.","To find how long a trip takes, count on. A bus leaves at 08:45 and gets in at 09:16. Count 15 minutes to 09:00. Then count 16 more. That is 31 minutes. Do not take 845 from 916. An hour has 60 minutes, not 100.","A two-way table sorts things two ways at once, like year group and favourite sport. Each total adds up its own row or column. If a cell is missing, take the other cells away from the total."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 10. In the National Curriculum in England, Year 4 statistics asks children to interpret and present data in tables, and Year 5 asks them to complete, read and interpret information in tables, including timetables. Timetables also practise elapsed time across the hour (Year 4 measurement; CCSS 3.MD.1). This leads into pictograms and tally charts, using several sources, and two-way tables with relative frequencies.","The skill is locating a cell from two headings and then using it: reading a time, choosing a bus by its arrival time, or working out a duration. Timetables are usually written in the 24-hour clock (14:05 is 2:05 pm), and a dash or blank means a service does not call at that stop.","Common mistakes: reading the right column but the wrong row, such as the Library time for Market Square. Choosing a bus by when it leaves when the question gives a time to arrive by. Taking times away as if they were ordinary numbers: 09:16 − 08:45 gives 71, not 31. In two-way tables, children often add the total into the row instead of taking the other cells away from it.","In the lab your child taps cells in a bus timetable with four stops to answer questions. They work around the dash and drag a marker along a time line to count on past an o'clock. They find the latest bus that still arrives in time. Then they fill the gaps in a two-way table about favourite sports and check that the row and column totals agree."]},"familyTip":"Next time you take a bus or train, or look one up online, read the timetable together. Ask: which column is our bus? Which row is our stop? If we must be there by a set time, which is the latest one we can catch, and how long will the trip take?","keyIdea":"A table sorts information into rows and columns. In a bus timetable each column is one bus and each row is one stop, so you go down the bus's column to the stop's row and read the time. A dash means the bus does not stop there. To find how long a trip takes, count on to the next o'clock and then add the rest, because an hour has 60 minutes, not 100. In a two-way table, each total adds up its own row or column, so you can work out a missing number.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each question need one column (one bus) or one row (one stop)? Drag each card, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["read down one bus's column","read along one stop's row"],"items":[["every stop Bus 3 makes",0],["every bus that leaves Park Road",1],["when Bus 1 reaches each stop",0],["the first bus at the Library",1],["how long Bus 2 takes from start to end",0],["the last bus at the Station",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtimetab","caption":"Flagship lab: read a bus timetable by column and row, meet the dash, count on to time a trip, find the latest bus, and fill a two-way table.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
